```
ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: [
  "Sample-based type inference is a textbook hazard: any finite-sample type sniffer that commits to a narrowest-fitting type without a re-widen pass will, by construction, silently coerce out-of-sample values that fit the inferred storage type (int accepts 3 via truncation of 3.14 at the C cast level). The loud-vs-silent asymmetry is the trivial consequence of CAST<INT64>(double) being a defined lossy operation in C/SQL semantics while CAST<INT64>('hello') is undefined → throws. No novel mechanism: it is the expected behavior of strtoll-style parsing composed with a non-revising sniffer.",
  "The 4/4 sweep at positions {20481, 25000, 30000, 39000} is not independent evidence of prevalence — it is one phenomenon (post-sample boundary) probed four times; n_effective ≈ 1."
]
CONFOUNDS: [
  "Documented-promise-violation framing conflates 'structural errors' (row/column/dialect) with 'value-domain errors' (float-in-int-column). The doc quote is about structural parsing; truncation is a value cast, arguably outside the promised scope. Without a doc quote that explicitly covers value-cast fidelity, the 'contract violation' claim is overstated.",
  "Lineage control vs pandas is weak: pandas uses a two-pass / chunked inference with upcasting; comparing a one-pass sniffer to a multi-pass inferencer doesn't isolate 'DuckDB-novel' — it isolates 'one-pass vs multi-pass,' a known design-axis difference.",
  "Asymmetry attributed to 'DuckDB knows how to error' may actually be 'libc/Arrow/duckdb's numeric cast path silently truncates by default while the string→int path raises' — i.e., asymmetry lives in the cast layer, not a deliberate sniffer policy choice."
]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: [
  "Not tested: behavior with sample_size set to non-default-but-finite values (e.g., 100k, 1M) — does the silent-truncation regime persist proportionally, or is 20480 special? Without this, 'the default IS the silent knob' is under-supported.",
  "Not tested: HUGEINT, DECIMAL, DATE, TIMESTAMP columns under the same shape — is the asymmetry float-specific or general to all narrowing casts? Generality claim needs ≥2 more dtypes.",
  "Not tested: whether `auto_detect=false` with explicit BIGINT reproduces identical silent truncation (would prove the sniffer is irrelevant and the issue is cast semantics, demoting the finding to 'CAST is lossy, news at 11').",
  "Not tested: prior DuckDB versions (1.0/1.1/1.2/1.3/1.4) — is this a regression, longstanding, or recently introduced? Affects novelty and whether it's already on a fix track."
]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: [
  "n=1 per condition; no repeat trials, no variance bars (deterministic outcome expected, but should be stated, not assumed).",
  "No corpus-level prevalence estimate. 'Generic file shape' is asserted, not measured. A real prevalence claim requires sampling N public CSVs and measuring frequency of (integer-early, fractional-late) pattern — this is L1 work, but the L0 prevalence prose currently outruns the L0 evidence.",
  "Issue-tracker search reported as null but the search query/scope is not in-packet; a single missed GitHub issue (e.g., titled 'sniffer narrows to BIGINT, later doubles silently rounded') would collapse this to RED. The negative-search needs to be auditable."
]
RATIONALE: The L0 is clean and the asymmetry is real, but two things keep this off GREEN: (1) the loud-vs-silent asymmetry is mechanically the expected behavior of CAST<int64>(double) vs CAST<int64>(string) composed with a non-re-widening sniffer — i.e., predicted by basic type-system theory, not an emergent surprise; the assumption-untested probe `auto_detect=false` with explicit BIGINT would discriminate 'sniffer bug' from 'cast semantics' and is the cheapest decisive experiment. (2) The 'documented-promise violation' rests on reading 'structural errors' to cover value-cast fidelity, which is a stretch without a tighter doc citation. Advance to L1 ONLY if: (a) the auto_detect=false probe shows silent truncation persists (then it's a cast-layer issue, downgrade), or fails to reproduce (then sniffer-specific, upgrade); (b) generality across ≥1 other narrowing cast (DECIMAL, DATE) is shown; (c) a tighter doc quote covering value-fidelity is found, OR the framing is moved from 'contract violation' to 'undocumented-behavior-with-asymmetric-diagnostics.' Without these, this is a well-characterized known hazard with a specific instantiation — file an issue, write a short note, but not yet a publishable cross-area phenomenon. Prompt v001.
